# V3·RB1 — Fetching by hand: races, aborts, stale answers

The archive version of this sector. It restates the concepts with worked code,
lists the mistakes in the order they are usually made, gives the interview
answer, and finishes with a question bank.

## 1. The state machine

The default shape people reach for:

```tsx
const [data, setData] = useState<User | null>(null);
const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
```

Three independent variables, eight combinations, three of them meaningful.
The reachable-but-meaningless ones are not hypothetical: `loading` true with
`error` set happens the moment you start a retry without clearing the error,
and `data` set with `error` set happens whenever a refetch fails.

The union version:

```tsx
type Resource<T> =
  | { status: "idle" }
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "success"; data: T; fetchedAt: number }
  | { status: "error"; error: Error };
```

Now `state.data` does not exist outside `success`, and the render function is
a `switch` the compiler checks for exhaustiveness. Add a `never` default and
adding a fifth variant becomes a compile error at every consumer, which is the
behaviour you want from a refactor.

Attach a request id to every action:

```tsx
type Action<T> =
  | { type: "start"; id: number }
  | { type: "ok"; id: number; data: T }
  | { type: "fail"; id: number; error: Error };

function reducer<T>(state: State<T>, action: Action<T>): State<T> {
  if (action.id !== state.requestId && action.type !== "start") return state;
  // ...
}
```

This is the race guard moved out of the effect and into the state itself,
which means it protects every caller — including event handlers, where no
cleanup runs.

## 2. The transport wrapper

```tsx
export class HttpError extends Error {
  constructor(readonly status: number, readonly body: unknown) {
    super(`HTTP ${status}`);
    this.name = "HttpError";
  }
}

export async function fetchJson<T>(url: string, init?: RequestInit): Promise<T> {
  const res = await fetch(url, init);
  const text = await res.text();
  const body = text ? JSON.parse(text) : null;   // 204 has no body
  if (!res.ok) throw new HttpError(res.status, body);
  return body as T;
}
```

Three details that are easy to get wrong. Reading `text()` first means a 500
that returns HTML does not blow up in `res.json()` with a parse error that
hides the status. A `204 No Content` has an empty body and `JSON.parse("")`
throws. And you may only read the body once — `res.json()` after `res.text()`
throws `TypeError: body stream already read`.

Aborts pass straight through: `fetch` rejects with a `DOMException` named
`AbortError` before any of this runs.

## 3. Loading versus fetching

```tsx
const isLoading = state.status === "loading";      // nothing to show
const isFetching = state.status === "success" && state.revalidating;
```

Rendering rule:

```tsx
if (isLoading) return <Skeleton />;
return (
  <div aria-busy={isFetching}>
    <Table rows={state.data} />
  </div>
);
```

The user keeps reading the table; `aria-busy` tells assistive technology that
an update is in flight without moving focus or announcing anything disruptive.

When *should* you clear? When the identity of the thing changed. Navigating
from user 7 to user 9 and leaving user 7's name on screen under a spinner is
worse than a skeleton — it is wrong information presented as right. Refetching
user 7 and leaving user 7 on screen is exactly right.

## 4. Composed cancellation

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const signal = AbortSignal.any([controller.signal, AbortSignal.timeout(5000)]);

  fetchJson<User>(`/api/users/${id}`, { signal }).then(
    (user) => { if (!signal.aborted) setUser(user); },
    (err: unknown) => {
      if (controller.signal.aborted) return;             // we walked away
      if (err instanceof DOMException && err.name === "TimeoutError") {
        setError(new Error("The server took too long."));
        return;
      }
      setError(err as Error);
    },
  );

  return () => controller.abort();
}, [id]);
```

Note the asymmetry: the *guard* checks the composed signal, but the *silence*
check reads `controller.signal` specifically, because a timeout is news and an
unmount is not.

## 5. Retry with backoff, abort-aware

```tsx
function sleep(ms: number, signal: AbortSignal): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (signal.aborted) return reject(signal.reason);
    const timer = setTimeout(resolve, ms);
    signal.addEventListener("abort", () => {
      clearTimeout(timer);
      reject(signal.reason);
    }, { once: true });
  });
}

export async function withRetry<T>(
  attempt: (signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<T>,
  { signal, tries = 3, base = 200 }: { signal: AbortSignal; tries?: number; base?: number },
): Promise<T> {
  let lastError: unknown;
  for (let i = 0; i < tries; i++) {
    signal.throwIfAborted();
    try {
      return await attempt(signal);
    } catch (err) {
      if (signal.aborted) throw err;
      if (!isRetryable(err)) throw err;
      lastError = err;
      const delay = base * 2 ** i * (0.5 + Math.random());   // jitter
      await sleep(delay, signal);
    }
  }
  throw lastError;
}
```

`isRetryable` is a policy, and writing it down is the point: transport errors
(`TypeError`) yes; `HttpError` with 408, 429 or any 5xx yes; every other status
no. `{ once: true }` on the abort listener matters when the same signal is
reused across attempts — otherwise you accumulate listeners and Node warns
about a leak at eleven.

## 6. In-flight deduplication

```tsx
const inFlight = new Map<string, Promise<unknown>>();

export function dedupe<T>(key: string, run: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T> {
  const existing = inFlight.get(key) as Promise<T> | undefined;
  if (existing) return existing;
  const promise = run().finally(() => { inFlight.delete(key); });
  inFlight.set(key, promise);
  return promise;
}
```

The `finally` is the whole design. Without it you have built a cache with an
infinite TTL and no invalidation, and you will spend an afternoon wondering
why the page never updates after a deploy.

One subtlety: `run().finally(...)` returns a *new* promise, and that is the one
callers get. If a caller aborts, everybody sharing the promise is affected —
which is why deduped requests should carry their own controller, not the
caller's signal, or should be reference-counted. The sector's exercise keeps to
the simple version and names the limitation.

## 7. Parallel requests and partial failure

```tsx
const controller = new AbortController();
const { signal } = controller;

const [profile, activity] = await Promise.all([
  fetchJson<Profile>(`/api/users/${id}`, { signal }),
  Promise.allSettled([fetchJson<Activity[]>(`/api/users/${id}/activity`, { signal })]),
]);
```

`Promise.all` rejects as soon as one input rejects, but the others keep
running: rejection is not cancellation. If the required call fails and you want
the optional one stopped, `controller.abort()` in the catch.

## 8. Paged accumulation

Two invariants: pages append in request order, and a change to the query
resets the accumulator. Both fall out of a generation counter:

```tsx
const gen = useRef(0);

async function loadMore() {
  const myGen = gen.current;
  const page = await fetchPage(cursor, signal);
  if (myGen !== gen.current) return;         // query changed underneath us
  setPages((prev) => [...prev, page]);
}

// whenever the query changes:
gen.current += 1;
setPages([]);
```

## Common mistakes, ranked by how often they ship

1. Reporting `AbortError` to the user.
2. Not checking `res.ok`, then debugging a parse error two files away.
3. Clearing data at the start of every fetch, producing spinner flash.
4. Retrying a 401 or a 422 — three times as many failures, same outcome.
5. Sleeping between retries without an abort listener.
6. Caching a settled promise in the dedupe map.
7. `await`ing two independent requests in sequence.
8. Guarding `.then` and forgetting `.catch`.
9. Building the cache key with `JSON.stringify` over an object whose key order
   varies.
10. Calling `setState` from a `finally` that runs after unmount, then silencing
    the warning instead of fixing the cause.

## The interview answer

*"How do you handle race conditions in React data fetching?"* The answer that
lands has three levels, delivered in this order. First the mechanism: promises
have no cancel, so the callback attached to a superseded request will run, and
whether it does damage depends entirely on whether it can still reach
`setState`. Second the tools: an effect's cleanup gives you a per-run box to
flip; a click handler has no cleanup, so you need a monotonic request id;
`AbortController` goes further and stops the work rather than the answer.
Third, the dissolution: if answers are stored under the question that produced
them — keyed by id, by page, by query — a late answer lands in its own slot and
cannot be rendered by accident, and the race stops existing rather than being
defended against. Mention that `fetch` resolves on 404 and that aborting shows
up as a rejection you must not report, and you have covered everything the
question was actually testing.

## Check yourself

1. Name the four terminal outcomes of a `fetch` call and how each one surfaces.
2. Why does `res.json()` throw after `res.text()`?
3. What is the difference between `AbortError` and `TimeoutError`, and who
   produces each?
4. Give a concrete UI where clearing data on refetch is correct, and one where
   it is wrong.
5. Why can a `useRef` boolean guard wedge a component, while a `let` inside the
   effect cannot?
6. Which HTTP statuses are worth retrying, and why is `POST` special?
7. What does jitter protect against, and who is harmed without it?
8. Why must the dedupe map delete its entry in a `finally`?
9. `Promise.all` rejects on the first failure. What happens to the other
   requests?
10. Write a cache key for `{ page: 2, sort: "name" }` that does not depend on
    property order.
11. Why does `aria-busy` beat swapping in a spinner during a background
    refetch?
12. In StrictMode your effect runs twice in development. Which of the patterns
    in this sector make that harmless, and which one would double-charge a
    `POST`?
13. A generation counter lives in a ref rather than state. Why?
14. What breaks if the dedupe map hands the caller's `signal` to the shared
    request?

