## Exercise 2 — the transport wrapper and the error taxonomy

Edit `ex2/solution.tsx`. Export an error class and a fetch wrapper.

```tsx
export class HttpError extends Error {
  readonly status: number;
  readonly body: unknown;
}

export async function fetchJson<T>(
  url: string,
  init?: RequestInit,
): Promise<T>;
```

`fetchJson` must:

- call `fetch(url, init)` exactly once;
- read the body **once**, as text, and parse it as JSON only when the text is
  non-empty — a `204` or an empty `200` resolves to `null`;
- resolve with the parsed body when `res.ok`;
- reject with an `HttpError` carrying `status` and the parsed body when it is
  not — and still parse the body, because a `422` carries the validation
  errors you need;
- when the body is not valid JSON *and* the response was ok, reject with a
  plain `Error` whose message contains `Invalid JSON`; when the body is not
  valid JSON and the response was **not** ok, still reject with an `HttpError`
  whose `body` is the raw text;
- let an abort rejection out untouched — the gate aborts a request and asserts
  the rejection is a `DOMException` named `AbortError`, not an `HttpError`.

`HttpError` instances must satisfy `err instanceof HttpError`,
`err instanceof Error`, and `err.name === "HttpError"`.

The gate injects a fake `fetch` via `globalThis.fetch`, so call the global —
do not import anything.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rb01_fetching_by_hand ex2
```

