## Challenge — `useResource`, assembled

Edit `challenge/solution.tsx`. Build the hook the previous eight exercises
were parts of.

```tsx
export type ResourceState<T> = {
  data: T | null;
  error: Error | null;
  isLoading: boolean;   // no data yet and a request is out
  isFetching: boolean;  // a request is out, with or without data
  refetch: () => void;
};

export function useResource<T>(options: {
  key: string | null;
  load: (signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<T>;
  timeoutMs?: number;
  tries?: number;
}): ResourceState<T>;
```

Everything below must hold at once:

1. `key === null`: no request, `data` stays `null`, `isLoading` is `false`.
2. A key change is a **different resource**: `data` clears immediately and
   `isLoading` becomes `true`.
3. `refetch()` on the same key is a **refresh**: `data` stays, `isFetching`
   becomes `true`, `isLoading` stays `false`. Calling it while a request is
   already out must not start a second one.
4. Every request gets a signal composed from an unmount/supersession controller
   and `AbortSignal.timeout(timeoutMs ?? 8000)`. A timeout sets `error` to an
   `Error` whose message contains `timed out`; a supersession or unmount sets
   nothing.
5. Failures other than aborts are retried up to `tries ?? 2` attempts using
   `withRetry` and `isRetryable` from `../ex5/solution`, with the same signal —
   so an unmount during a backoff sleep stops the loop.
6. Answers are guarded by a request sequence number kept in a ref, so an answer
   from a superseded request cannot land. A successful answer clears any
   previous error; a failed one keeps the previous `data` **only** when the
   failure was a refetch of a key whose data is already on screen.
7. No state updates after unmount.

The gate walks all seven, using fake timers and out-of-order resolution.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rb01_fetching_by_hand challenge
```

