## Exercise 1 — the entry, and derived staleness

Edit `ex1/solution.tsx`. No React in this file.

```tsx
export type Entry<T = unknown> = {
  key: string;
  data: T | undefined;
  error: Error | undefined;
  updatedAt: number;
  isFetching: boolean;
};

export function blankEntry(key: string): Entry;

export function isStale(entry: Entry, staleTime: number, now?: number): boolean;

export function entryStatus(
  entry: Entry | undefined,
): "missing" | "loading" | "success" | "error" | "refetching";
```

- `blankEntry` returns `{ key, data: undefined, error: undefined, updatedAt: 0,
  isFetching: false }`.
- `isStale` is `true` when `updatedAt === 0` (never successfully loaded) or when
  `now - updatedAt > staleTime`. `now` defaults to `Date.now()`. A `staleTime`
  of `Infinity` means never stale once loaded; a `staleTime` of `0` means stale
  immediately after the millisecond it was written — note the strict `>`.
- `entryStatus`:
  - `undefined` entry → `"missing"`;
  - `isFetching` with no `data` → `"loading"`;
  - `isFetching` with `data` → `"refetching"`;
  - `error` set and no `data` → `"error"`;
  - `data` set → `"success"` (even when `error` is also set — a failed
    revalidation leaves good data on screen).

That last rule is the one the gate cares about most: it builds an entry with
both `data` and `error` and expects `"success"`.

