## Hint 1

Prefix matching per segment is the detail that makes `serializeKey` a
non-trivial choice. If a key is one flat string, `key.startsWith(prefix)` will
happily match `"ordersArchive"` against `"orders"`. Serialise each segment
separately and join with a separator that cannot appear inside a segment — or
store the segments and compare them as arrays.

## Hint 2

Canonical serialisation is a recursion with one extra line:

```tsx
function canon(value: unknown): unknown {
  if (Array.isArray(value)) return value.map(canon);
  if (value && typeof value === "object") {
    return Object.fromEntries(
      Object.keys(value as object).sort().map((k) => [k, canon((value as never)[k])]),
    );
  }
  return value;
}
export const serializeKey = (parts: readonly unknown[]) =>
  parts.map((p) => JSON.stringify(canon(p))).join("�");
```

Sorting the keys is what makes `{a,b}` and `{b,a}` the same query. The null
byte is a separator no realistic key segment contains.

## Hint 3

`updatedAt: 0` rather than `delete` is the load-bearing decision, and the gate
checks it: deleting throws away data the user is currently reading and replaces
it with a spinner, for a mutation that may not even have changed this
particular entry. Marking it stale means the next `ensureData` refetches it
while it stays on screen.

The eager/lazy split is what makes invalidation affordable — an app with 200
cached pages and one visible component sends one request, not 200.

