## Challenge — `createQueryClient` and `useQuery`

Edit `challenge/solution.tsx`. Assemble the sector.

```tsx
export function createQueryClient(options?: {
  staleTime?: number;
  gcTime?: number;
  now?: () => number;
}): QueryClient;

export type QueryClient = {
  getQueryData<T>(key: readonly unknown[]): T | undefined;
  setQueryData<T>(key: readonly unknown[], updater: T | ((prev: T | undefined) => T), at?: number): boolean;
  invalidateQueries(prefix: readonly unknown[]): void;
  fetchQuery<T>(key: readonly unknown[], fetcher: () => Promise<T>): Promise<T>;
  subscribe(key: string, listener: () => void): () => void;
  getEntry(key: string): Entry | undefined;
};

export function useQuery<T>(
  client: QueryClient,
  key: readonly unknown[],
  fetcher: () => Promise<T>,
  options?: { staleTime?: number; enabled?: boolean },
): {
  data: T | undefined;
  error: Error | undefined;
  isLoading: boolean;
  isFetching: boolean;
  refetch: () => void;
};
```

Everything must hold at once:

1. Keys are serialised canonically (ex5) — property order never produces a
   miss.
2. `useQuery` binds through `useSyncExternalStore` (ex2) and re-renders only for
   its own key.
3. On mount: a fresh entry produces **no request**; a stale or missing one
   produces exactly one, even with three components mounted on the same key.
4. `data` survives a failed refetch; `error` sits beside it (ex1, ex3).
5. Successful writes go through `structuralShare` (ex6) — a refetch returning
   identical data leaves `data` referentially unchanged.
6. Entries are evicted `gcTime` after the last unmount, and a remount inside
   that window is instant (ex4).
7. `invalidateQueries` marks matches stale and refetches only the subscribed
   ones (ex5).
8. `setQueryData` refuses writes older than the entry (ex8).
9. `enabled: false` means no request and no subscription-driven fetch;
   flipping it to `true` fetches.

The gate exercises all nine plus StrictMode.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rb02_query_client challenge
```

