## Exercise 3 — `use()` and the promise you must not recreate

Edit `ex3/solution.tsx`. Export a cache and a component.

```tsx
export function getCityWeather(
  city: string,
  fetchWeather: (city: string) => Promise<string>,
): Promise<string>;

export function clearWeatherCache(): void;

export function Weather({
  city,
  fetchWeather,
}: {
  city: string;
  fetchWeather: (city: string) => Promise<string>;
}): JSX.Element;
```

- `getCityWeather` returns the **same promise object** for the same city until
  the cache is cleared. `getCityWeather("oslo", f) === getCityWeather("oslo", f)`
  must hold.
- `Weather` calls `use(getCityWeather(city, fetchWeather))` and renders the
  result in `<p>`.
- A rejected fetch must **not** be cached — the next render should retry.
  Delete the entry when the promise rejects.

The gate renders `<Weather city="oslo" />` inside a boundary, resolves it,
re-renders five more times with the same props, and asserts `fetchWeather` was
called **once**. It then switches to `city="lima"` and asserts a second call
and a fallback. Finally it renders a component that calls `use()` on a promise
created inline during render, asserts the fallback never resolves and the
fetcher is called repeatedly — you write that broken version too, exported as
`BrokenWeather`, because seeing the loop is the lesson.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rb03_suspense_streaming ex3
```

exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

The rule is: **`use()` caches nothing about your request; it only knows promise
identity.** Two promises that fetch the same URL are two different things to
React. So something outside the render has to guarantee that the same question
yields the same promise object, and a module-level `Map` keyed by the question
is the smallest thing that does.

## Hint 2

```tsx
const cache = new Map<string, Promise<string>>();

export function getCityWeather(city, fetchWeather) {
  let promise = cache.get(city);
  if (!promise) {
    promise = fetchWeather(city);
    cache.set(city, promise);
  }
  return promise;
}
```

Note where the `set` happens: synchronously, before returning, so a second
caller in the same tick finds it.

## Hint 3

Not caching rejections needs care, because the obvious version changes what the
caller receives:

```tsx
promise = fetchWeather(city);
promise.catch(() => cache.delete(city));    // side channel, does not alter `promise`
cache.set(city, promise);
```

Attaching `.catch` for its side effect and storing the *original* promise is
deliberate — `cache.set(city, promise.catch(...))` would store a promise that
fulfils with `undefined`, and the component would render nothing instead of
suspending again.

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rb03_suspense_streaming / ex3 - reference solution.
import { use, type JSX } from "react";

// Outside render, because `use()` knows nothing about your request - only
// promise identity. Two promises fetching the same URL are two different
// things to React.
const cache = new Map<string, Promise<string>>();

export function getCityWeather(
  city: string,
  fetchWeather: (city: string) => Promise<string>,
): Promise<string> {
  const existing = cache.get(city);
  if (existing) return existing;

  const promise = fetchWeather(city);

  // Attached for its side effect only; the ORIGINAL promise is what gets
  // stored and returned. Storing `promise.catch(...)` would store a promise
  // that fulfils with undefined, and the component would render nothing
  // instead of suspending again on retry.
  promise.catch(() => {
    if (cache.get(city) === promise) cache.delete(city);
  });

  // Written synchronously, before returning, so a second caller in this same
  // tick finds it rather than starting a parallel request.
  cache.set(city, promise);
  return promise;
}

export function clearWeatherCache(): void {
  cache.clear();
}

export function Weather({
  city,
  fetchWeather,
}: {
  city: string;
  fetchWeather: (city: string) => Promise<string>;
}): JSX.Element {
  const summary = use(getCityWeather(city, fetchWeather));
  return <p>{summary}</p>;
}

/**
 * The bug, on purpose. Render creates promise A and suspends. A settles, React
 * re-renders, render creates promise B and suspends. The fallback never
 * leaves, the network fills with identical requests, and React issues no
 * warning - it cannot tell this apart from a legitimate wait.
 */
export function BrokenWeather({
  city,
  fetchWeather,
}: {
  city: string;
  fetchWeather: (city: string) => Promise<string>;
}): JSX.Element {
  const summary = use(fetchWeather(city));
  return <p>{summary}</p>;
}

// v3rb03_suspense_streaming / ex4 - reference solution.
import { Suspense, use, useState, useTransition, type JSX } from "react";

const cache = new Map<string, Promise<string>>();

function getArticleCached(
  id: string,
  getArticle: (id: string) => Promise<string>,
): Promise<string> {
  const existing = cache.get(id);
  if (existing) return existing;
  const promise = getArticle(id);
  promise.catch(() => {
    if (cache.get(id) === promise) cache.delete(id);
  });
  cache.set(id, promise);
  return promise;
}

function ArticleBody({
  id,
  getArticle,
  busy,
}: {
  id: string;
  getArticle: (id: string) => Promise<string>;
  busy: boolean;
}): JSX.Element {
  const body = use(getArticleCached(id, getArticle));
  return <article aria-busy={busy ? "true" : "false"}>{body}</article>;
}

export function ArticleBrowser({
  ids,
  getArticle,
}: {
  ids: string[];
  getArticle: (id: string) => Promise<string>;
}): JSX.Element {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();

  const select = (id: string) => {
    // The STATE UPDATE is what gets marked non-urgent. startTransition knows
    // nothing about the fetch; it tells React that a render which suspends
    // should keep the committed UI rather than falling back.
    startTransition(() => setSelected(id));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {ids.map((id) => (
        <button
          key={id}
          type="button"
          onClick={() => select(id)}
          // Deliberately NOT disabled while pending. Staying interactive
          // during non-urgent work is the entire point of a transition;
          // disabling reproduces the blocking behaviour we were avoiding.
          disabled={false}
        >
          {id}
        </button>
      ))}

      {selected === null ? null : (
        <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading article</p>}>
          <ArticleBody id={selected} getArticle={getArticle} busy={isPending} />
        </Suspense>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

// v3rb03_suspense_streaming / ex5 - reference solution.
import { memo, useDeferredValue, useId, useState, type JSX } from "react";

// memo is load-bearing, not decoration: the parent re-renders on every
// keystroke, and re-rendering a parent re-renders its children regardless of
// whether their props moved. Without this, the deferral achieves nothing.
export const ExpensiveList = memo(function ExpensiveList({
  query,
  items,
  renderCount,
}: {
  query: string;
  items: string[];
  renderCount?: { current: number };
}): JSX.Element {
  if (renderCount) renderCount.current += 1;

  const needle = query.toLowerCase();
  const matches = items.filter((item) => item.toLowerCase().includes(needle));

  return (
    <ul>
      {matches.map((item) => (
        <li key={item}>{item}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
});

export function FilterList({
  items,
  renderCount,
}: {
  items: string[];
  renderCount?: { current: number };
}): JSX.Element {
  const inputId = useId();
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");

  // The live value drives the input - urgent, never delayed. The deferred copy
  // drives the expensive subtree. Deferring the input's own value is the
  // classic mistake and produces exactly the laggy typing it was meant to fix.
  const deferredQuery = useDeferredValue(query);
  const stale = query !== deferredQuery;

  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor={inputId}>Filter</label>
      <input
        id={inputId}
        value={query}
        onChange={(event) => setQuery(event.target.value)}
      />

      {/* True exactly while the screen shows results for an older query:
          "this is updating", not "this is wrong". */}
      <div aria-busy={stale ? "true" : "false"} data-stale={stale ? "true" : "false"}>
        <ExpensiveList query={deferredQuery} items={items} renderCount={renderCount} />
      </div>
    </div>
  );
}

exercises.hints:
## Hint 1

Two independent timers governed by two different questions, and it helps to
name them. The *delay* timer answers "has this been slow long enough to be
worth mentioning?" and runs while the flag is hidden. The *floor* timer answers
"has this been visible long enough not to look like a glitch?" and runs while
the flag is shown. They are never both running.

## Hint 2

One effect with two branches, keyed on `active` and the current flag:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (active && !visible) {
    const t = setTimeout(() => { shownAt.current = now(); setVisible(true); }, delayMs);
    return () => clearTimeout(t);
  }
  if (!active && visible) {
    const remaining = minVisibleMs - (now() - shownAt.current);
    if (remaining <= 0) { setVisible(false); return; }
    const t = setTimeout(() => setVisible(false), remaining);
    return () => clearTimeout(t);
  }
}, [active, visible, delayMs, minVisibleMs]);
```

`shownAt` is a ref because it is written from inside a timer and never
rendered.

## Hint 3

The 210ms row of the table is the whole argument for the second threshold, so
check it deliberately. With only a delay: nothing for 200ms, spinner appears,
response lands at 210ms, spinner vanishes — a 10ms flash, which reads as a
glitch rather than as information. With the floor: the spinner appears at 200ms
and stays until 600ms, which reads as a system doing something.

Compute `remaining` from a recorded timestamp rather than tracking elapsed time
yourself, or a re-render in the middle of the visible window resets the clock.

references.solution.tsx:
// v3rb03_suspense_streaming / ex6 - reference solution.
import { useEffect, useRef, useState, type JSX } from "react";

/**
 * Two thresholds, from Miller (1968) / Nielsen (1993): below ~100ms a system
 * feels instantaneous and an indicator is noise; an element that appears and
 * vanishes inside ~300ms reads as a glitch rather than as information.
 */
export function useDelayedFlag(
  active: boolean,
  {
    delayMs = 200,
    minVisibleMs = 400,
    now = Date.now,
  }: { delayMs?: number; minVisibleMs?: number; now?: () => number } = {},
): boolean {
  const [visible, setVisible] = useState(false);

  // Written from inside a timer, never rendered.
  const shownAt = useRef(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Hidden and busy: has this been slow long enough to be worth mentioning?
    if (active && !visible) {
      const timer = setTimeout(() => {
        shownAt.current = now();
        setVisible(true);
      }, delayMs);
      // Covers the third case people forget: work finishes DURING the delay.
      // Nothing was shown, so nothing needs a floor - just cancel.
      return () => clearTimeout(timer);
    }

    // Shown and idle: has it been visible long enough not to look like a
    // glitch?
    if (!active && visible) {
      const remaining = minVisibleMs - (now() - shownAt.current);
      if (remaining <= 0) {
        setVisible(false);
        return;
      }
      const timer = setTimeout(() => setVisible(false), remaining);
      return () => clearTimeout(timer);
    }

    // Hidden and idle, or shown and busy: nothing to schedule.
    return undefined;
  }, [active, visible, delayMs, minVisibleMs, now]);

  return visible;
}

export function SaveIndicator({ saving }: { saving: boolean }): JSX.Element | null {
  const show = useDelayedFlag(saving);
  if (!show) return null;
  return <p role="status">Saving</p>;
}

