## Exercise 7 — ordered reveal without `SuspenseList`

Edit `ex7/solution.tsx`. React 19 does not ship `SuspenseList`; build the
behaviour you need from nesting.

```tsx
export function OrderedPanels({
  panels,
}: {
  panels: { id: string; resource: Resource<string> }[];
}): JSX.Element;

export function UnorderedPanels({
  panels,
}: {
  panels: { id: string; resource: Resource<string> }[];
}): JSX.Element;
```

`UnorderedPanels` renders each panel in its own sibling boundary, fallback
`<p>Loading {id}</p>`. `OrderedPanels` renders them so that panel *n+1* can only
appear after panel *n* has appeared, using **nested** boundaries.

Each revealed panel is `<section aria-label={id}>{resource.read()}</section>`.

The gate resolves three panels in the order **c, a, b**, and asserts:

- with `UnorderedPanels`, `c` appears first, above `a` and `b`'s fallbacks —
  reveal order is settle order, not tree order;
- with `OrderedPanels`, nothing appears until `a` settles; then `a` alone;
  then, when `b` settles, `a` and `b`; then all three. `c` settling first
  reveals nothing.

Reveal order matters when the content is a narrative or a document; it does not
when the content is a grid of independent cards. Your `OrderedPanels` should
have a comment naming that trade — the total time to full reveal becomes the
sum rather than the maximum of the settle times.

```
uv run dojo test react v3rb03_suspense_streaming ex7
```

