## Review — Suspense and errors are a pair

`review1/solution.tsx`. A boundary that catches "not yet" and one that catches
"it failed" belong together, and the retry story is where hand-written versions
break.

```tsx
export class RetryBoundary extends Component<
  { children: ReactNode; fallback: (props: { error: Error; retry: () => void }) => ReactNode },
  { error: Error | null; attempt: number }
>;

export function Panel({
  id,
  load,
}: {
  id: string;
  load: (id: string, attempt: number) => Promise<string>;
}): JSX.Element;

export function ResilientPanel({
  id,
  load,
}: {
  id: string;
  load: (id: string, attempt: number) => Promise<string>;
}): JSX.Element;
```

- `RetryBoundary` catches errors from its subtree via
  `getDerivedStateFromError`, renders `fallback({ error, retry })`, and on
  `retry()` clears the error **and increments `attempt`**.
- The children are rendered under `key={attempt}`, so a retry remounts the
  subtree — without that, a component holding a poisoned cached promise
  re-renders and throws again immediately.
- `Panel` reads `use(cached(id, attempt, load))`, where the cache key includes
  the attempt number, so a retry genuinely asks again.
- `ResilientPanel` composes: `RetryBoundary` outside, `Suspense` inside,
  fallback `<p>Loading</p>`, error fallback `<div role="alert">` containing the
  message and a `Retry` button.
- Resetting must also clear the failed cache entry, or the new attempt number
  is the only thing that saves you and a shared cache leaks entries forever.

The gate fails the first load, asserts the alert, clicks `Retry`, asserts the
fallback reappears and `load` was called with `attempt` `1`, resolves, and
asserts the content. It then fails twice in a row and asserts the attempt
counter reaches `2`.

