# V3·RB4 — Optimistic updates and rollback

The archive version: the model, the code, the four hard problems, the mistakes,
the interview answer and a question bank.

## 1. The layer model

Do not think of an optimistic update as writing to state. Think of it as
pushing an entry onto a list of pending transformations:

```tsx
type Pending<T> = { id: string; apply: (value: T) => T };

const view = pending.reduce((acc, p) => p.apply(acc), confirmed);
```

Everything follows:

- **Show**: push an entry.
- **Confirm**: remove the entry, and let the server's value become `confirmed`.
- **Roll back**: remove the entry. Nothing else is touched, because nothing else
  was overwritten.
- **Concurrency**: two entries; removing one leaves the other applied.

Compare the overwrite model, where the optimistic value replaces `confirmed`
and rollback means restoring a snapshot. Under overwrite, the information
needed to undo *one* change without undoing another has already been destroyed
at the moment you wrote it.

## 2. Snapshot rollback, and exactly where it breaks

```tsx
async function toggle(id: string) {
  const snapshot = items;                 // the whole list, before
  setItems((prev) => prev.map((i) => (i.id === id ? { ...i, done: !i.done } : i)));
  try {
    await api.toggle(id);
  } catch {
    setItems(snapshot);                   // correct — for exactly one mutation
  }
}
```

Now click two toggles 50ms apart. Toggle A snapshots `[a:false, b:false]`.
Toggle B snapshots `[a:true, b:false]`. B succeeds. A fails, restores its
snapshot, and B's confirmed change vanishes. The user's successful action was
undone by an unrelated failure, with no message.

The functional-update version of rollback fixes the common case:

```tsx
catch {
  setItems((prev) => prev.map((i) => (i.id === id ? { ...i, done: !i.done } : i)));
}
```

Undoing *the specific change* rather than restoring *the whole state*. That is
the layer model expressed in a single line, and it is the right instinct even
before you build the full machinery.

## 3. `useOptimistic`

```tsx
const [optimisticLikes, addOptimisticLike] = useOptimistic(
  likes,
  (current: number, delta: number) => current + delta,
);

function onLike() {
  startTransition(async () => {
    addOptimisticLike(1);          // visible immediately
    await api.like(postId);        // …until this transition settles
    setLikes(await api.getLikes(postId));
  });
}
```

Two rules, and both catch people:

**It only works inside a transition or an action.** Outside one, React
re-renders with the base state on the very next render and your optimistic
value is gone before the user sees it. The symptom is "useOptimistic does
nothing".

**The optimistic layer is discarded when the transition ends**, not when you
say so. So the underlying state must have been updated by then, or the value
snaps back to the old one for one frame — a visible flicker. In a Server
Actions setup the revalidation handles it; by hand, update the real state
inside the same transition, before it resolves.

## 4. `useActionState`

```tsx
const [state, formAction, isPending] = useActionState(
  async (_prev: State, formData: FormData): Promise<State> => {
    const title = String(formData.get("title") ?? "");
    if (!title.trim()) return { error: "Title is required" };
    try {
      await api.create({ title });
      return { error: null, savedAt: Date.now() };
    } catch (e) {
      return { error: (e as Error).message };
    }
  },
  { error: null },
);

return (
  <form action={formAction}>
    <input name="title" />
    <button disabled={isPending}>Add</button>
    {state.error && <p role="alert">{state.error}</p>}
  </form>
);
```

What it buys you over a hand-written submit handler: the action's return value
*is* the new state, so success and failure cannot get out of sync; `isPending`
is managed; the form is progressively enhanceable; and successive submissions
are sequenced rather than raced. Note that the action never throws — errors
become part of the returned state, which is what keeps them renderable.

## 5. Identity

```tsx
// Good: the key never changes.
const clientId = crypto.randomUUID();
setItems((prev) => [...prev, { clientId, serverId: null, title }]);
const saved = await api.create({ id: clientId, title });
setItems((prev) =>
  prev.map((i) => (i.clientId === clientId ? { ...i, serverId: saved.id } : i)),
);

// Bad: the key changes when the server answers.
<li key={item.serverId ?? item.tempId}>
```

The bad version remounts the row at the moment of confirmation. Everything the
DOM owns — focus, an open menu, an uncontrolled input, a running transition —
is lost, and any entrance animation replays. Users describe it as "the item
flickers when it saves".

Sending the client id as the record's id has a second benefit: the write is
idempotent. A retry after a lost response cannot create a duplicate, which is
what makes retrying a `POST` safe.

## 6. Ordering

```tsx
const version = useRef(0);
const applied = useRef(0);

async function setStatus(next: Status) {
  const v = ++version.current;
  setOptimistic(next);
  const confirmed = await api.setStatus(next);
  if (v < applied.current) return;      // an older response, arriving late
  applied.current = v;
  setStatus(confirmed);
}
```

The counter-intuitive part is that you must drop *successful* responses too.
A successful response for a superseded request is still describing an old
world. Applying it because "it worked" is exactly how a toggle ends up in the
state the user did not choose.

That handles your own races. It does not handle another user changing the same
record — for that you need a server-supplied version and a policy: reject with
409 and let the user re-apply, or last-writer-wins, stated explicitly rather
than by accident.

## 7. Undo instead of confirm

```tsx
function remove(id: string) {
  setHidden((prev) => new Set(prev).add(id));           // gone from the UI
  const timer = setTimeout(() => {
    void api.remove(id);                                // …and now from the server
    setHidden((prev) => { const n = new Set(prev); n.delete(id); return n; });
    setItems((prev) => prev.filter((i) => i.id !== id));
  }, 5000);
  setUndo({ id, timer });
}

function undo() {
  clearTimeout(undoState.timer);
  setHidden((prev) => { const n = new Set(prev); n.delete(undoState.id); return n; });
}
```

The item never leaves the list until the window closes, so undo restores its
exact position for free — with a delete-then-restore design you have to
remember the index, and you have already sent a request that may have
succeeded.

## 8. Optimistic writes into a cache

```tsx
const snapshot = client.getQueryData<Todo[]>(["todos"]);
client.setQueryData<Todo[]>(["todos"], (prev = []) => [...prev, optimistic]);

try {
  const saved = await api.create(optimistic);
  client.setQueryData<Todo[]>(["todos"], (prev = []) =>
    prev.map((t) => (t.clientId === optimistic.clientId ? saved : t)),
  );
} catch {
  client.setQueryData<Todo[]>(["todos"], snapshot);
} finally {
  client.invalidateQueries(["todos"]);
}
```

Two hazards. The rollback must not revert a *newer* legitimate write, which is
what the previous sector's `updatedAt` guard is for. And you must cancel any
in-flight query for that key before writing, or a response that left before
your mutation will land after it and overwrite the optimistic row.

## Common mistakes, ranked

1. Restoring a whole-state snapshot and undoing a concurrent success.
2. `useOptimistic` outside a transition — "the hook does nothing".
3. Changing a row's `key` when its server id arrives.
4. Applying a successful response from a superseded request.
5. Reverting silently, with no announcement, so nobody notices the failure.
6. `role="alert"` for a failed background save (hostile) or nothing at all for
   a failed payment (dangerous).
7. Not cancelling in-flight reads before an optimistic cache write.
8. Optimistically updating something irreversible — a payment, an email send.
9. Mutating the cached array in place, so React sees no change and the
   snapshot points at the mutated object.
10. A confirmation dialog where an undo window would do.

## The interview answer

*"How would you implement an optimistic update?"* Start with the model rather
than the code: keep the confirmed server state and a list of pending
transformations, and render the fold of the second over the first. That gives
you rollback (drop one entry) and concurrency (two entries, independent) for
free, where a snapshot-and-restore approach undoes unrelated successes. Then
name the three things that still bite. Identity: the React key must not change
when the temporary id becomes a real one, which is why client-generated ids are
worth the argument — they double as idempotency keys, so a retried write cannot
duplicate. Ordering: responses can arrive out of order, so stamp mutations with
a version and drop late ones, *including successful ones*. And communication: a
silent revert is a bug, so announce politely and offer a retry in place. Finish
with when not to: anything irreversible or high-stakes should be pessimistic,
because "we showed it as done and it was not" is much worse for a payment than
a 300ms wait.

## Check yourself

1. Give the exact sequence where snapshot rollback destroys a successful
   concurrent update.
2. What does `useOptimistic` require in order to work at all?
3. When is the optimistic layer discarded?
4. Why does changing a row's key on confirmation lose focus?
5. What makes a client-generated id an idempotency key?
6. Why must you drop *successful* responses from superseded requests?
7. What can a server version do that a client counter cannot?
8. Why is undo usually better than a confirmation dialog?
9. Where should the deleted item live during an undo window, and why?
10. What must you do to in-flight reads before writing optimistically to a
    cache?
11. `role="alert"` versus `aria-live="polite"`: which for a failed autosave?
12. Name two mutations that should never be optimistic.
13. What is the flicker that happens when the base state has not caught up by
    the time a transition ends?
14. How does the functional-updater form of rollback approximate the layer
    model?

exercises.prompt:
## Exercise 1 — snapshot rollback, and the row it destroys

Edit `ex1/solution.tsx`. Export two components; the contrast is the exercise.

```tsx
type Flag = { id: string; label: string; on: boolean };

export function SnapshotFlags({
  initial,
  save,
}: {
  initial: Flag[];
  save: (id: string, on: boolean) => Promise<void>;
}): JSX.Element;

export function LayeredFlags({ initial, save }: { /* same */ }): JSX.Element;
```

Both render one `<li>` per flag with a `<button>` labelled
`{label}: {on ? "on" : "off"}` that toggles it optimistically and calls
`save(id, nextValue)`.

- `SnapshotFlags` captures the whole list before the mutation and restores that
  array on failure. Write it exactly that way — it is the version you will
  find in most codebases.
- `LayeredFlags` rolls back by applying the **inverse of that one change** with
  a functional update, touching nothing else.

The gate clicks flag `a`, then flag `b`, resolves `b` successfully, then
rejects `a`, and asserts:

- `SnapshotFlags` ends with **both** flags back at their original values —
  `b`'s successful toggle was destroyed;
- `LayeredFlags` ends with `a` reverted and `b` still toggled.

Both must render `<p role="status">Could not save {label}</p>` on failure.

