## Exercise 2 — read the form, do not mirror it

Edit `ex2/solution.tsx`. Export a reader and a form. No `useState` for field
values anywhere in this file.

```tsx
export type Signup = {
  email: string;
  plan: string;
  interests: string[];
  newsletter: boolean;
  referrer: string | null;
};

export function readSignup(form: HTMLFormElement): Signup;

export function SignupForm({ onSubmit }: { onSubmit: (data: Signup) => void }): JSX.Element;
```

The form contains, all uncontrolled with `defaultValue`/`defaultChecked`:

- `email` — a text input labelled `Email`;
- `plan` — a `<select>` labelled `Plan` with `free`/`pro`, defaulting to `free`;
- `interests` — a `<select multiple>` labelled `Interests` with `code`,
  `design`, `ops`;
- `newsletter` — a checkbox labelled `Newsletter`, unchecked by default;
- `referrer` — a text input labelled `Referrer` with **no** default, which must
  read as `null` when empty rather than `""`.

`readSignup` must handle the two `FormData` traps: an unchecked checkbox is
**absent**, not `false`; and a multiple-selection field needs `getAll`, because
`get` returns only the first and `Object.fromEntries` keeps only the last.

`SignupForm` submits with `onSubmit={e => { e.preventDefault();
onSubmit(readSignup(e.currentTarget)); }}`.

The gate submits untouched (asserting the defaults and `newsletter: false`,
`referrer: null`), then selects two interests and checks the box and asserts
both survive.

