## Exercise 8 — registering every kind of control

Edit `ex8/solution.tsx`. Export a value reader and a tiny form hook.

```tsx
export type FieldValue = string | boolean | string[] | FileList | null;

export function readControl(
  el: HTMLInputElement | HTMLSelectElement | HTMLTextAreaElement,
): FieldValue;

export function useMiniForm(
  initial: Record<string, FieldValue>,
): {
  values: Record<string, FieldValue>;
  register: (name: string) => {
    name: string;
    onChange: (event: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement | HTMLSelectElement | HTMLTextAreaElement>) => void;
  };
};
```

`readControl` must handle the controls that do not behave like text:

- `<select multiple>` → an **array** of selected values, from
  `selectedOptions`;
- `type="checkbox"` → the **boolean** `checked`, not the string `value` (which
  is `"on"` by default, regardless of state);
- `type="radio"` → the `value` when `checked`, and `null` when not, so an
  unselected radio does not clobber the group;
- `type="file"` → the `files` `FileList`, or `null`;
- everything else → `value`.

`useMiniForm.register(name)` returns props to spread onto a control. Its
`onChange` writes `readControl(event.target)` into `values[name]` — **except**
that a `null` from an unchecked radio must leave the existing value alone.

The gate builds a form with a text input, a checkbox, a radio group of three, a
`<select multiple>`, and a file input, drives each one, and asserts the exact
value shapes — including that toggling a checkbox off yields `false` and not
`"on"`, and that selecting radio `b` after radio `a` yields `"b"` and not
`null`.

